From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:36:41 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0704): forgetting a peer ends its sessions and their
 cached trust

AC-39, AC-43 (whole-product review finding 9). main_remove_peer only deleted the file; a
session to that peer kept its in-memory pin, host ID and remembered password, and a retrying
session could reconnect on the stale pin and re-save the password from handle_peer_info.

Each peer now has a forget generation (remotesmith_trust::forget). A session takes it when it
starts and writes the peer's config only while it is current, checked and written under one
lock. Forgetting bumps the generation, closes the peer's live sessions for good (no retry, a
connect in flight is dropped, the window says why) after dropping their in-memory copy, then
deletes the file. A stale session that reaches the identity gate reloads the (now empty)
config and drops its remembered password first, so whatever connects next enrolls afresh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
